Jahshaka patch 0075 (claimed 0074 in the ATOM-3 lane; renumbered at merge —
0074 is PHOTON-M1's composite) — a LOD switch has a hysteresis band, and THE
BAND BELONGS TO THE PASS.

AMENDED IN PLACE 2026-09-17 (lane ATOM-3-FIX, after the Fable read of ATOM-3,
ledger §664 finding 1). The first version of this patch made the band
PROCESS-WIDE (`LodStrategy::setHysteresis`) and used `MovableObject::
mCurrentMeshLod` as its direction state. Both are wrong for the same reason:
ONE SCENE IS RENDERED BY MANY PASSES AND THEY DO NOT SHARE A CAMERA. The
planar reflector's mirrored camera (OgrePlanar.cpp), a picture-in-picture
inset (OgreChain.cpp's buildPip), a reflection-probe cube face and a thumbnail
are all `pass_scene` definitions on the same SceneManager with
`mUpdateLodLists` true and no LOD camera of their own, so each of them runs
`updateAllLods` over the same objects in the same frame and each wrote
`mCurrentMeshLod`. A band that read that slot therefore measured the watched
view's value against ANOTHER camera's level: it could hold a level the view
never chose (whenever the two cameras sit within a band of one threshold), and
its memory of the direction of travel was erased by every sibling pass, so the
band stopped suppressing the pops it exists for in any scene that has a mirror
or an inset. Measured both ways in spikes/atom-3/FINDINGS.md §7.

THIS VERSION: the band is a PER-PASS PARAMETER with its own per-object state.

  * `CompositorPassSceneDef::mLodHysteresis` — 0 by default, which is upstream
    to the bit (`lodSet` is then the same `lower_bound` it always was);
  * threaded through `SceneManager::updateAllLods` (a defaulted 5th argument,
    so no other caller in the engine or in a sample changes) and
    `UpdateLodRequest::lodHysteresis` into `LodStrategy::lodUpdateImpl` and
    `LodStrategy::lodSet`, beside `lodBias` which travels the same road;
  * `MovableObject::mHysteresisLod` — the LAST LEVEL A BANDED PASS CHOSE, and
    nothing else. Only a pass with a band writes it, so a zero-band sibling
    cannot move it; 0xFF means "no banded pass has seen this object", which is
    what makes the first frame of any capture upstream's answer exactly. It
    costs no memory: it lands in the padding between `mCurrentMeshLod` and
    `mMinPixelSize`.

SOURCE patch. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after
pulling this.

FILES, and the one overlap to know: `OgreLodStrategy.h`,
`OgreLodStrategyPrivate.inl`, `OgreMovableObject.h/.cpp`, `OgreRenderable.h`
(the two `friend` declarations name `lodSet` and `lodUpdateImpl` BY SIGNATURE),
`OgreDistanceLodStrategy.h/.cpp`, `OgrePixelCountLodStrategy.h/.cpp` (the
three upstream strategies pass the band through — no behaviour of theirs
changes, and a pin bump that adds a fourth strategy will fail to compile
loudly rather than silently ignoring the band), `OgreSceneManager.h/.cpp`,
`OgreCompositorPassSceneDef.h` and `OgreCompositorPassScene.cpp`. Of these
only `OgreMain/src/OgreSceneManager.cpp` is touched by another patch in this
stack (0016 at line ~1620 and 0053 at ~1081, far from this patch's ~2126), so
a tree that already carries those two can report this patch as "unapplied" on
`build-ogre.sh`'s per-patch reverse check — the documented overlap class
(CLAUDE.md, 2026-09-14). Judge by content; the cure is always the submodule
reset recipe before `build-ogre.sh`.

WHY A PATCH AND NOT A WORKAROUND (owner rule, 2026-09-15, ledger §325).
`LodStrategy::lodSet` picks a level with ONE `std::lower_bound` against the
mesh's value array and writes `MovableObject::mCurrentMeshLod`. The comparison
is a step in both directions, so an object whose LOD value sits ON a threshold
changes level EVERY FRAME the value dithers — a camera breathing at a switch
distance, a dolly crawling across one, a hand-held pose in a headset. The band
cannot be written outside the pin:

  * `mCurrentMeshLod` and `mLodMesh` are PROTECTED on `MovableObject`
    and the friendship it grants is to `LodStrategy::lodSet` and
    `LodStrategy::lodUpdateImpl` BY NAME (OgreMovableObject.h:354-356) —
    friendship to a base class's virtual member does not extend to a derived
    class's override, so a strategy of our own (which is how the rest of
    ATOM-3's view rule is written, with no patch at all) can compute a VALUE
    but can never see or hold the level;
  * a hysteresis expressed in the value alone needs the direction of travel,
    i.e. per-object state, and `ObjectData` has no slot for it;
  * `getCurrentMeshLod()` is public, but without the mesh's thresholds — which
    are the protected `mLodMesh` — knowing the current level says nothing about
    how far the value is from the switch;
  * and the per-pass half of it lives in `CompositorPassScene::execute`, which
    is the only caller of `updateAllLods` and passes the pass definition's own
    numbers (`mLodBias` already travels exactly this way).

WHAT IT DOES. With a band `h`, a switch to a COARSER level requires the value
to pass the crossed threshold by `h * |threshold|` and a switch back to a
FINER one requires it to fall below by the same, so the level only moves when
the picture would genuinely change and a value that is genuinely past the band
moves on the frame it gets there. The band is RELATIVE, so it means the same
thing in every strategy's units (distances, pixel counts, or Jahshaka's
world-space errors), and it can only ever hold the CURRENT level — a
multi-level jump still jumps all the way, and nothing can be held for more
than the band's width of travel.

MEASURED (ATOM-3, spikes/atom-3/FINDINGS.md §4), in FRAMES and not in seconds:
one chained mesh alone in the scene, the camera oscillating sinusoidally about
its level-1 switch distance (3.836 m), 600 frames, a level change counted every
frame the drawn triangle count moves. The oscillation crosses the threshold 71
times in those 600 frames.

  amplitude   band 0 (upstream)   band 0.10 (ours)
  +-2 %       71 pops, 2 levels   0 pops, 1 level
  +-20 %      71 pops, 2 levels   71 pops, 2 levels

So the band removes exactly the pops that are noise — every crossing of a
motion too small to change the picture — and keeps every crossing of a motion
that is real.

WHO SETS IT (ATOM-3-FIX): `chain::build` writes `ChainDesc::lodHysteresis`
onto EVERY `PASS_SCENE` of the view's own node (the same sweep shape as
`applyStereo` and `maskOutHelpers`, and for the same reason — every scene pass
in that node shares the view's camera, so a band on some of them and not
others would draw one level in the opaque pass and another in the refraction
or overlay pass). `OgreView::chainDesc()` sets 0.10 for a view a person watches
over time — an on-screen view, and the VR session's stereo view, whose target
is offscreen but whose picture is the wearer's — and 0 for every capture:
thumbnails, previews, screenshots and every pixel suite therefore take the
exact level their own value asks for, frame after frame, which is what keeps
them assertable. The planar, probe, shadow and PiP node definitions are built
by other functions and never see the sweep, so they are 0 by construction.
`JAHSHAKA_NO_LOD_HYSTERESIS` still zeroes the band run-wide for an A/B, and
`JAHSHAKA_LOD_HYSTERESIS_OFFSCREEN` grants it to offscreen views for the one
suite that measures it (`engine.lod_hysteresis`: an offscreen view is the only
kind whose pixels a test can read).

ONE CONSEQUENCE TO KNOW, unchanged: with a band the measured switch distance
depends on the direction of approach, by the width of the band — the same
bisection reads 3.836 m with it off and 4.047 m with it on. Every LOD probe
sequence dollies OUTWARD.

Verified: `git apply --check` against the pristine pin, and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying.
